---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const seo = {
  title: "Solar Pricing in California",
  page_description: "Real, unfiltered solar pricing data for California homeowners.",
  no_index: false,
};
---

<Layout title={seo?.title || "Solar"} seo={seo}>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 prose">
    <h1>California Solar Pricing Guide 2024</h1>
    <p>We believe in transparent pricing. Here is what you should actually expect to pay for a solar and battery system in California right now.</p>

    <h2>Average Cost Per Watt (Solar Only)</h2>
    <p>In California, the fair market price for a standard residential solar installation (before the 30% tax credit) is between <strong>$2.70 and $3.50 per watt</strong>.</p>
    <ul class="tabular-nums">
      <li><strong>5 kW System:</strong> $13,500 - $17,500</li>
      <li><strong>8 kW System:</strong> $21,600 - $28,000</li>
      <li><strong>12 kW System:</strong> $32,400 - $42,000</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Battery Pricing (Mandatory for NEM 3.0)</h2>
    <p>Batteries are expensive but necessary to achieve a reasonable ROI under the new Net Billing Tariff. Prices below are fully installed.</p>
    <ul class="tabular-nums">
      <li><strong>1x Tesla Powerwall 3 (13.5 kWh):</strong> ~$13,500 - $15,500</li>
      <li><strong>2x Tesla Powerwalls (27.0 kWh):</strong> ~$22,000 - $25,000</li>
      <li><strong>Enphase IQ 5P (5.0 kWh):</strong> ~$5,500 - $6,500 per unit</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Example NEM 3.0 System Quote</h2>
    <p>A typical California home with a $250/month electric bill requires a system similar to this:</p>
    <table class="w-full tabular-nums text-left border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr class="bg-gray-100">
          <th class="p-2 border">Item</th>
          <th class="p-2 border">Cost</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="p-2 border">7.2 kW Solar Array (18 x 400W panels @ $3.00/W)</td>
          <td class="p-2 border">$21,600</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="p-2 border">1x Tesla Powerwall 3</td>
          <td class="p-2 border">$14,500</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="font-bold">
          <td class="p-2 border">Gross System Cost</td>
          <td class="p-2 border">$36,100</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="text-[var(--accent-primary)]">
          <td class="p-2 border">Federal ITC (30%)</td>
          <td class="p-2 border">-$10,830</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="font-bold text-xl">
          <td class="p-2 border">Net Cost</td>
          <td class="p-2 border">$25,270</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 mt-8 rounded border border-gray-200">
      <h3>Beware the "Dealer Fee"</h3>
      <p>If you are financing your system through a solar loan (e.g., GoodLeap, Mosaic) with a very low interest rate (like 3.99%), the installer is paying a "dealer fee" to the bank, which is passed on to you. This fee can add 20% to 35% to the gross cost of the system. <strong>Always ask for the cash price vs the financed price.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
